yoyo -p hi extra no longer drops extra without a word. It now exits 2, names the word, and sends nothing to the model. Committed as 0a3305e0 ("Fixes #1150"). fmt, clippy with -D warnings, the build and the full cargo test all passed.

Probe before and after (built binary, local stub):

| Command | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| -p hi extra / extra -p hi / --model x -p hi y | sent only hi | exit 2, names the word, nothing sent |
| --system --model claude-zzz -p hi (the #1114 repro) | sent hi, claude-zzz vanished | exit 2, names claude-zzz |
| -p hi -- extra, -p "hi extra" | sent hi extra | unchanged |
| -p hi doctor | refused as a misplaced subcommand | unchanged; that check runs earlier, so the two never both fire |

The message: `error: word 'extra' is not part of -p's value and would be dropped. Quote the whole prompt (-p "hi extra") or put the extra words after -- (-p "hi" -- extra). Nothing was sent to the model.`

• Who could break: every script and workflow that runs yoyo passes the prompt on stdin. The one that uses --prompt (humaneval_one.sh) quotes it. None of them gets refused, so I went with a refusal rather than a warning.
• Where the code lives: the logic is in src/cli_config.rs (stray_prompt_words and stray_prompt_word_refusal). src/cli.rs only adds 2 lines next to the unknown-flag refusal, so the check runs before the trust prompt or any request. The module_size test passes without raising cli.rs's size entry.
• New tests: a unit table with near-miss rows (quoted prompt, --, a flag's value, empty -p, no -p, control bytes), plus real-binary rows in tests/flag_value_slot.rs. The #1114 value-slot check keeps its own row without a stray word.
• Positive control: with the check replaced by a no-op, exactly the two refusal tests failed by name. I restored it; no sabotage marker remains.
• Old tests: three tests had the old dropping built in, and I rewrote them rather than deleting them. One asserted that "ignored" was dropped. One had a name claiming -p takes precedence. One used --system --thinking high -p hi, where high is now a stray word, so it runs without -p and checks the same thing.
• Docs: docs/src/usage/single-prompt.md now says -p takes one word: quote the prompt or put the rest after --.

Two mistakes on the way:
• Twice I tried to stop the probe stub with a pkill/pgrep -f pattern that also matched my own shell command, which killed that command before the commit ran.
• My commit message file at /tmp/msg.txt had been overwritten by Task 1's message. So my diff first landed under Task 1's title, which says "Part of #1122". I caught it right away and amended my own unpushed commit with the correct title and body.

#1150 has no comments, so there were no extra open rows to clear before using "Fixes".
